      "section": "290.0123 subd. 1 and subd. 5",
      "confidence": "primary",
      "note": "Tax Foundation's table shows the same single and joint amounts, though its footnote (vv) on the Minnesota row describes a different, smaller deduction that does not match the DOR figures. Phase-out rule (290.0123 subd. 5): the deduction is reduced by the lesser of (1) 3% of AGI over the first threshold but not over the second, plus 10% of AGI over the second threshold, or (2) 80% of the deduction. Above a third threshold the reduction is a flat 80%. The DOR's 2026 thresholds are $244,400, $337,800 and $1,107,750, the same for single, joint and head of household. The percentages are from the statute and the thresholds from the DOR's inflation-adjusted amounts. The points are generated from that rule: full deduction up to $244,400, deduction minus $2,802 at $337,800, and 20% of the deduction from the income where the 80% cap is reached ($432,180 single, $554,580 joint, $493,780 head of household), which is below $1,107,750 in every case, so the flat 80% rule adds nothing. PolicyEngine implements the same formula. The statute says \"adjusted gross income\"; it is entered as federal AGI. The extra deduction for age 65 or blindness is not in the schema, so the schedule covers the base deduction only.",
